Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
all right now to the latest on the war  in ukraine defense forces are preparing  for a ground assault by russian troops  in the donesk industrial region of  eastern ukraine the local governor there  says that there is no safe place left  and is urging the remaining 350 000  residents to leave  it's a desperate rush to leave  but saying goodbye  is hard  there isn't much left for them here in  the city of slovyansk russian shelling  has demolished homes small businesses  and hopes for aging with dignity and  security  this was my shop here  i have no words lots of money lots of  resources invested  20 years of work  everything is lost  i have no more income  nothing connects us to this city anymore  i'm scared for my wife and my daughter  who is six months pregnant  they need to leave here  especially after what happened yesterday  they hit the city center  russia has already claimed victory in  nearby luhansk now the kremlin is moving  the front line of the war to the front  doorstep of donetsk in the donbass  region  two civilians died after shelling struck  a market in slovyansk earlier this week  the local government is begging  civilians all 350 000 of them to  evacuate  i have no more choice tomorrow  i will join the army  this isn't the first time war has come  to slovyansk it was seized by  russian-backed separatists in 2014 and  recaptured two and a half months later  there's no telling how long these  refugees will have to stay away  or what they'll find when they return  ukraine's military is continuing the  fight  as russia's forces advance  let's go now to our correspondent nick  connolly he is in key following events  for us nick we know that russian forces  have control of half of that critical  dawnbass region that they say that they  want to take what's the latest now on  the offensive to take the remaining half  well there's real fear here in kiev that  russia is on something of a role after  weeks spent taking severe donetsk and  huge losses they took so donetsk's  sister city of less chance in a matter  of days so there's a sense that they  could really be pushing home that  advantage that their new strategy of  focusing their efforts on a pretty small  section the front lines moving away from  trying to do too much what they did in  the early months of this war that failed  so spectacularly that's now really  bringing fruit that you know russia is  outgunning and outmanning basically  ukraine in this conflict and uh that for  now ukraine doesn't have the resources  isn't getting the western support that  it's been asking for and that ukraine's  western partners simply don't really  understand the scale and the intensity  of this war and that all these  deliveries are just going to be too  little too late the only kind of little  shimmer of hope are reports now in the  last few days from moscow that some of  these units might now be taken back into  the russian kind of hinterland to take a  break to re-group and that you know the  kind of tiredness and exhaustion after  months pretty intense fighting is also  uh you know being felt by the russian  troops and that might give kiev a bit of  a chance to bring those western weapons  to the front lines and kind of even  things out a bit  nick we know that residents have been  told to leave the city of sloviansk it  comes just a day after at least two  people were killed  in a russian attack on the market there  are our residents are they heeding the  warning to leave are they able to leave  well as far as we understand it the  figure is all a bit disparate but the  majority of the pre-war population of  ukraine controlled donbass we understand  have already left in the previous weeks  and months most of the people have  stayed not too far away gone 100 200  kilometers from the front lines but as  you mentioned there about 300 000 people  still believed to be there and on the  whole we've seen time and time again  people leaving it too late people saying  i have nowhere to go i don't have  resources no one wants to host me and  basically people wait until they can  hear the shelling in their own homes to  leave by which time it's often too  dangerous and they end up  either losing their own lives or  endangering the people trying to help  them so this time the government is  trying to get in there earlier be more  drastic in its warnings also put more  pressure on people to get out to avoid  them losing their lives and also  endangering the people who then will be  at worst you know in worst case sent in  to help them so that's their hope now  and definitely an expectation here in  kiev that more cities are going to fall  to the russians before ukraine is able  to turn the tables and to start going  back into counterfeit attack  our very own nick connolly with the  ladies tonight from kiev in ukraine nick  as always thank you  ukraine's government estimates that more  than 10 000 of its soldiers have been  killed  in the conflict the international  observers fear that the  actual figure is much higher  some of those wounded are being treated  in hospitals in other european countries  ivan hunchenko got out alive  was badly wounded  defending kiev against russian troops  we discovered them in a forest  we immediately began fighting  we were lucky that day because our unit  left the battle with only two wounded  me and another lad  hunchenko received two bullet wounds and  a shrapnel wound to his legs  he is one of the first ukrainian  soldiers to be treated in germany since  the russian attack began  the medical care the patient received in  ukraine was excellent but he needed  further operations of course to do that  we needed a large team in the  appropriate infrastructure which is  practically non-existent in ukraine at  the moment  six soldiers are being treated at the  louisiana hospital in argen  maxim katavenko was shot at by a russian  tank in the donbas a piece of shrapnel  hit him in the arm  my median nerve is damaged  i can move my hand in this direction but  not in the other  and i can't feel anything in these two  fingers  even if there is peace in germany the  doctors in achen are familiar with war  injuries  we had a lot of experience treating  patients from iraq many years ago  so in principle this is nothing new for  us  but it's not just doctors helping the  wounded  ukrainians who live in argent stopped by  today to wish even a happy birthday  he just turned 37  a brief and welcome moment of  destruction from the war  it's hard to read the news about ukraine  here you are safe and everything is good  planes and helicopters fly and you  aren't afraid  in ukraine it's completely different  everything that flies belongs to your  country's army or that of the enemy  that's scary no it's a natural  so far about 300 people from ukraine  have been flown to germany for treatment  including civilians and soldiers  ivan hunchenko knows one thing for sure  he wants to return and continue fighting  you

